    "difficulty": "Mastery",
    "difficultyReason": "Relates a radial increase to a circumference increase, reconciles metres and kilometres, and explains why the result is independent of the original diameter."
  },
  "content": {
    "id": "p48-q9",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "9",
    "prompt": "Suppose a belt tightly stretched around the equator of the earth just fits.\nThe diameter of Earth is $12\\,755\\text{ km}$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p48-q9-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "How much longer should you make the belt so that the belt could encircle the earth at a distance of $1\\text{ m}$ away at all points?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$2\\pi\\text{ m}$ more (approximately $6.28\\text{ m}$).",
          "worked": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p48-q9-a-answer-units",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "right",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Use metres. A 1 m increase in radius increases the diameter by 2 m.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p48-q9-a-answer-calculation",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "right",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\begin{aligned}d_{\\text{tight}}&=12\\,755\\,000\\text{ m}\\\\d_{\\text{loose}}&=12\\,755\\,002\\text{ m}\\\\C_{\\text{tight}}&=\\pi(12\\,755\\,000)\\text{ m}\\\\C_{\\text{loose}}&=\\pi(12\\,755\\,002)\\text{ m}\\\\C_{\\text{loose}}-C_{\\text{tight}}&=\\pi(12\\,755\\,002)-\\pi(12\\,755\\,000)\\\\&=2\\pi\\text{ m}\\end{aligned}",
                    "display": true
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 48
      },
      {
        "id": "p48-q9-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Explain why the answer to “how much longer should you make a belt such that it encircles $1\\text{ m}$ away from the original circumference” is always $2\\pi\\text{ m}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The diameter increases by $2\\text{ m}$, so the circumference increases by $\\pi\\times2=2\\pi\\text{ m}$, regardless of the original diameter.",
          "worked": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p48-q9-b-answer-definition",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "right",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Let the original diameter be ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "d",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " metres. Increasing the radius by 1 m increases the diameter by 2 m.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p48-q9-b-answer-calculation",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "right",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\begin{aligned}C_{\\text{original}}&=\\pi d\\text{ m}\\\\C_{\\text{new}}&=\\pi(d+2)\\text{ m}\\\\&=(\\pi d+2\\pi)\\text{ m}\\\\C_{\\text{new}}-C_{\\text{original}}&=2\\pi\\text{ m}\\end{aligned}",
                    "display": true
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p48-q9-b-answer-reason",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "right",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "The increase is always exactly ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "2\\pi\\text{ m}",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ", whether the original circle is a balloon or the Earth, because the original diameter cancels.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 42
      }
    ]
  },
